 "key_big": "Same base: set the exponents equal. Otherwise, take the log of both sides.",
 "key_sub": "log_b M = k means M = b^k. Check that every log argument is positive.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>If both sides can use the <b>same base</b>, set the exponents equal: 2<sup><i>x</i>+1</sup> = 2<sup>5</sup> gives <i>x</i> + 1 = 5.</li><li>Otherwise, isolate the power and <b>take the log of both sides</b>: 5<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 40 gives <i>x</i> = log 40 ÷ log 5. Use ln with base <i>e</i>.</li><li>To solve log<sub><i>b</i></sub>(<i>M</i>) = <i>k</i>, rewrite it as <i>M</i> = <i>b</i><sup><i>k</i></sup>. Combine logs with the properties first if there is more than one.</li></ul><p>Always check log equations: the argument of every log must be positive, so some answers are extraneous.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve 3 · 2<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 48.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 16 = 2<sup>4</sup></p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve log<sub>2</sub> <i>x</i> + log<sub>2</sub>(<i>x</i> − 2) = 3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i>(<i>x</i> − 2) = 8</p><p class='sn-ex-s'><i>x</i> = 4 or −2; −2 is extraneous</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 4</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Dividing the numbers instead of the logs: 5<sup><i>x</i></sup> = 40 does not give <i>x</i> = 8.</li><li>Taking logs before isolating the power, such as with 3 · 2<sup><i>x</i></sup>.</li><li>Keeping a solution that makes a log argument zero or negative.</li><li>Writing log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>x</i> = <i>k</i> as <i>x</i> = <i>kb</i> instead of <i>x</i> = <i>b</i><sup><i>k</i></sup>.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you solve an exponential equation?",
   "a": "If you can write both sides with the same base, set the exponents equal. Otherwise, isolate the power and take the log of both sides."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you solve a logarithmic equation?",
   "a": "Combine logs into one using the properties, rewrite in exponential form, solve, and check that every log argument is positive."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why can log equations have extraneous solutions?",
   "a": "Logs are only defined for positive numbers. An algebraic answer that makes an argument zero or negative must be rejected."
  },
  {
   "q": "How long does it take money to double?",
   "a": "Solve (1 + r)^t = 2 for annual compounding, or e^(rt) = 2 for continuous compounding: t = ln 2 ÷ r."
  }
 ],
